  "meta": {
    "title": "Fourth symbol from the end is a",
    "blurb": "A one-way DFA for this language needs 16 states, one for every possible last four letters. A two-way head does it in seven: run to the right endmarker, step back four cells, and look.",
    "inputs": [
      {
        "w": "abbb",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "aaaa",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "babab",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "bbabaa",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "abb",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "baaa",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "abbbb",
        "expect": "reject"
      }
    ],
    "library": {
      "author": {
        "login": "thethinkmachine"
      },
      "license": "CC-BY-4.0",
      "tags": [
        "two-way",
        "succinctness",
        "endmarkers"
      ],
      "difficulty": "intro",
      "readme": "Two-way finite automata recognise exactly the regular languages — the head can move back, but it cannot write, so there is nothing new it can remember. What it can buy is **size**.\n\nA one-way DFA for \"the *n*th symbol from the end is a\" must remember the last *n* symbols: 2ⁿ states. This machine walks to ⊣ and counts back *n* cells: *n* + 3 states. If the word is too short, the walk back reaches ⊢, where there is no move, and the word is rejected."
    }
  }
}
